    "description": "Online booking systems connect travel searches with supplier reservations, payments, and ticket or accommodation confirmations. In Iran, international payment access and the distinction between a platform and the actual service provider are particularly important to understanding these systems.",
    "tags": ["Online booking", "Travel technology", "Reservations", "Payments", "Travel infrastructure"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T22:57:15Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T22:57:15Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Online Travel Booking in Iran: Payments, Tickets and Confirmation",
      "description": "Understand online travel reservations in Iran, including supplier rules, international payment barriers, ticket issuance, and evidence limitations."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "booking-as-an-intermediary-service",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From search results to a reservation",
        "content": "An online travel system brings several activities into one interface: finding services, comparing options, requesting a reservation, and obtaining a confirmation. Those activities should not be confused with operating the hotel, bus, or airline itself. The inspected terms of 1stQuest describe its website as a means of gathering travel information and transacting with travel suppliers; separate supplier conditions also apply. This is a documented example of an intermediary model, not a complete description of Iran's booking market. [s1]\n\nThe distinction becomes concrete at ticket issuance. The same terms state that an airfare is guaranteed only after purchase is completed and tickets are issued. They also explain that separately booked one-way flights retain separate conditions. The editorial implication is straightforward: a search result, a payment record, and an issued ticket establish different things. A connected itinerary displayed together does not necessarily have one set of change or cancellation protections. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "advertised-booking-workflows",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What an advertised workflow establishes",
        "content": "1stQuest's bus page describes searching routes, comparing departures, paying online, and receiving an electronic ticket by email. It lists journeys between cities including Tehran, Kashan, Isfahan, Shiraz, and Yazd. These published pages demonstrate how the platform presents its service; they do not independently establish that seats on every advertised route can be purchased at a particular moment. [s2]\n\nIts transfer service illustrates another important boundary. The page describes bookings involving airports, hotels, bus terminals, and railway stations, but its FAQ limits the advertised service to transfers within a city. A platform can therefore cover several parts of a journey while leaving an intercity connection outside the product being purchased. Reading the actual service scope matters as much as recognizing a familiar destination name. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "payments-and-communications",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "International access has several layers",
        "content": "Payment access is a separate issue from whether a booking website can be opened. The UK Foreign, Commonwealth and Development Office reports that non-Iranian debit and cash cards do not work in Iran. That warning concerns access to money inside the country; it should not be expanded into an unsupported claim that every overseas intermediary uses the same payment arrangements. A platform advertising an online gateway has not, by that statement alone, demonstrated acceptance of a particular customer's card. [s4][s2]\n\nCommunications create another dependency. The same UK guidance documents past blocking, slowing, and shutdowns of internet and mobile services. For a reservation system, an email confirmation and a reliable way to retrieve it are consequently distinct parts of the travel process. Keeping a readable record of the supplier, service, date, and confirmation is a sensible operational inference from those documented communications risks, rather than evidence about any platform's reliability. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "comparison-and-coverage",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Comparing like with like",
        "content": "Even a price comparison needs a precise definition of the product. 1stQuest's published price guarantee requires matching service details and comparable cancellation conditions. That provides a useful illustration of why the lowest displayed amount alone is an incomplete comparison: itinerary, passenger scope, service class, and restrictions can change what is being sold. The guarantee remains a supplier's published policy, not an independently tested outcome. [s5]\n\nThe evidence inspected here supports an explanation of booking mechanics, but not a ranking of Iranian platforms. No purchase, refund, or payment acceptance was tested. Broader coverage requires primary documentation from additional providers and evidence on accessibility for foreign passport holders. Until then, advertised functions should remain clearly separated from verified transaction performance.",
        "source_ids": ["s5"]
      }
    ],
