    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Tourist transport in Iran involves both journeys between destinations and transfers connecting accommodation with terminals and stations. Railway heritage, commercial bus services, and local transfers reveal different parts of this system, while infrastructure alone does not establish current operating conditions.",
    "tags": ["Tourist transport", "Railways", "Intercity buses", "Local transfers", "Transport heritage", "Travel infrastructure"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T22:57:15Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T22:57:15Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Tourist Transport in Iran: Railways, Buses and Local Transfers",
      "description": "Explore Iran's transport infrastructure through railway heritage, advertised intercity buses, local transfers, and the limits of current service evidence."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "infrastructure-and-access",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A journey depends on connections",
        "content": "Transport for visitors includes several separate services. An intercity ticket connects destinations, while a local transfer connects a hotel with an airport, bus terminal, or railway station. Published 1stQuest service pages illustrate this distinction: its bus page advertises intercity routes, whereas its transfer FAQ describes an in-town service. Neither description demonstrates that an entire itinerary is available or protected as a single booking. [s2][s3]\n\nThis distinction helps explain travel infrastructure without turning a network map into a promise. A railway line, a listed bus route, and a bookable departure are different kinds of evidence. UNESCO's account establishes the historical significance of a major railway corridor; a commercial booking page establishes what a seller advertises. Actual operation requires more immediate confirmation than either source provides. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "railway-and-landscape",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The railway as infrastructure and heritage",
        "content": "The Trans-Iranian Railway links the Caspian Sea in the northeast with the Persian Gulf in the southwest. UNESCO describes a route crossing mountain ranges, rivers, forests, highlands, and plains. Construction began in 1927 and was completed in 1938. Its length of 1,394 kilometres refers to this specific heritage railway, not to the total extent of Iran's railway network. [s1]\n\nThe route's significance lies partly in the engineering needed to cross difficult terrain. UNESCO emphasizes bridges, tunnels, mountain cutting, and sustained gradients, alongside international construction cooperation and financing through national taxes. For a cultural account of transport, the railway is therefore more than a way to reach another destination: its alignment and structures document how a major national infrastructure project negotiated geography. Heritage recognition nevertheless supplies no current passenger timetable or guarantee of access to individual structures. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "buses-and-local-transfers",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Intercity services and the final connection",
        "content": "1stQuest advertises bus journeys linking several major visitor destinations, including Tehran, Isfahan, Shiraz, Kashan, and Yazd. Its FAQ distinguishes Standard and VIP products, describing the latter chiefly in terms of additional seating space and comfort. Such labels belong to the offered service: they should not be interpreted as a universal specification covering every operator, vehicle, or departure. [s2]\n\nAt the destination, the terminal-to-accommodation connection remains a separate task. The inspected transfer page advertises pickup and drop-off involving hotels and transport terminals and describes sedan, sport utility vehicle, and van options. Its explicit restriction to in-town transfers is important: an airport pickup product should not be assumed to include onward travel to another city. These details illustrate product boundaries rather than verified availability. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "operating-context",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Operating conditions matter as much as the network",
        "content": "The Canadian travel advisory dated 13 October 2026 advised against all travel to Iran, citing the volatile regional security situation, and noted that some airlines had suspended flights. Transport infrastructure should consequently not be presented here as an invitation to undertake an itinerary. That advisory is a Canadian government assessment, and its nationality-specific consular guidance must be understood in that context. [s4]\n\nThe same source identifies substantial road-safety concerns, checkpoints, and variation in road conditions. It recommends pre-booked official taxis and describes railway services as limited and slow. These observations provide context rather than route-level performance measurements. A responsible transport account must keep historical engineering, advertised services, and immediate operating conditions separate; the evidence inspected does not establish current schedules, accessibility, or the comparative safety of individual operators. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
